1. WHAT "THE WORK" MEANS

"The Work" is everything in this repository and everything derived from it:
the source code, the configuration files, the documentation, the research paper
in `paper/`, the figures and tables, the training logs, and any model weights or
checkpoints trained with this code (whether or not they are checked in).

"I", "me" and "the Author" mean Aditya Dutt Pandey.


2. WHAT YOU ARE ALLOWED TO DO (NO PERMISSION NEEDED)

a) Read the Work and learn from it.
b) Run the code unmodified on your own machine for personal study, evaluation,
   or non-commercial research, as long as you keep every copyright notice and
   this licence file intact.
c) Quote short excerpts (a few lines of code or a few sentences of text) in
   reviews, discussion, criticism, or teaching, with a clear credit and a link
   back to this repository. Citing the paper is always welcome (see
   `CITATION.cff`).


3. WHAT YOU ARE NOT ALLOWED TO DO (WITHOUT MY WRITTEN PERMISSION)

a) Copy, redistribute, publish, mirror, or re-upload the Work or any
   substantial part of it, in source or binary form.
b) Modify the Work, build derivative works from it, or fork it into a project
   of your own, other than private local edits you make to run option 2(b).
   Public forks on a code-hosting site are allowed only for the purpose of
   sending me a contribution (see section 6).
c) Use the Work, or anything derived from it, for any commercial purpose.
d) Use the Work to train, fine-tune, benchmark for publication, or build any
   other model or product, other than the private runs allowed in 2(b).
e) Remove, hide, or change any copyright notice, author credit, or this
   licence.


4. NO CLAIMING AUTHORSHIP, INCLUDING IN ACADEMIC SETTINGS

The Work was written by the Author. Nobody else may present it, or any part of
it, as their own work. This applies to everyone, and it is spelled out here on
purpose because it is the part most often ignored:

a) You may not submit the Work, or a lightly edited, renamed, translated,
   reformatted or "rewritten" version of it, as your own coursework,
   dissertation, thesis, project report, publication, patent filing,
   competition entry, or portfolio piece.
b) You may not list yourself, your institution, your lab, or your course as
   the author or owner of the Work.
c) Supervisors, examiners, reviewers and other people who see the Work
   during teaching, grading, or review get no ownership rights from that
   access. Seeing the Work, grading it, or advising on it does not make anyone
   an author or co-owner. Co-authorship or any shared credit exists only if I
   agree to it in writing, for that specific item.
d) Any use under section 2(c) or a written permission under section 7 must
   credit me as the original author.

If an institution wants to use the Work for teaching, hosting, or archiving, it
needs my written permission first (section 7).


5. WHAT IS *NOT* COVERED BY THIS LICENCE

This licence covers my own original expression: the code I wrote, my text, my
configurations, my figures, and my trained weights. It does not claim anything
that belongs to somebody else:

- The ideas and algorithms in the cited papers (masked diffusion, MDLM, D3PM,
  MaskGIT, LLaDA, DiT's adaLN-Zero, RoPE and so on) belong to their authors and
  are credited in the paper and the README. Nothing here claims to have
  invented them.
- Third-party libraries (PyTorch, Hugging Face `transformers` and `datasets`,
  `safetensors`, NumPy, etc.) keep their own licences.
- The TinyStories dataset and the pretrained tokenizers (`bert-base-uncased`,
  GPT-2) are downloaded from their original sources and keep their own terms.
  They are not part of this repository.


